\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Kernel complement and pseudodeterminant}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let #{L:E\to E} be a self-adjoint positive semidefinite operator on
 a finite real inner-product space #{(E,h)}. Set
##{\begin{aligned}
K&=\ker L,& H&=K^\perp,\\
k&=\dim K,& r&=\dim H.
\end{aligned}}
Write #{i:H\to E} for inclusion, #{p_H:E\to H} for orthogonal projection,
#{P_H=ip_H} and #{P_K=I-P_H} for the endomorphism projections.}
\p{The [spectral theorem](fdip-002R) gives #{E=K\mathbin\perp H}.
The positive-eigenvalue vectors span #{H}, so #{L(H)\subset H} and
#{L_H=L|_H:H\to H} is positive definite. Define the
\newvocab{pseudodeterminant} by
##{\operatorname{pdet}L=\det L_H
   =\prod_{\lambda_a>0}\lambda_a.}
Its empty product is one, including #{L=0}. These definitions use the
specified pairing and its [induced subspace volume](fdip-002M).}
\p{For a [weighted incidence Laplacian](fdip-0007) with positive real edge weights, the
[kernel theorem](fdip-0008) identifies #{K} with fields constant on each
connected component. Indeed its energy is a sum of positive weights
times squared edge differences; zero energy means every difference is
zero, and propagation along undirected paths gives those constants.
Loops contribute zero and isolated vertices are components. Thus #{H}
imposes zero sum separately on each component. For a connected nonempty
graph, #{n=|V|} gives the \newvocab{mean-zero field space}
##{\begin{aligned}
u_0&=\mathbf1_V/\sqrt n,\\
H&=\{\phi:\sum_v\phi(v)=0\},\\
P_H\phi&=\phi-\frac{\sum_v\phi(v)}{n}\mathbf1_V.
\end{aligned}}
The empty graph has #{E=K=H=0}; no division by #{n} is used there.}
\p{The kernel complement and determinant product are direct consequences
of the spectral decomposition in
\citet{Section A.5.2, pp. 646-647}{boyd2004convex}; the graph kernel
uses the incidence-energy identity proved in \ref{fdip-0008}.}

\p{The [massless four-cycle calculation](fdip-0038) makes this kernel
complement and pseudodeterminant explicit.}
